contains three sets of files:

1) The source program - together with its "include" files
2) Two IDL interface programs

There is also a subdirectory ./benchtest, which contains:
1) Files for several test runs
2) Atomic data files for H I, He I, and He II (in the "new" format of input).
3) Script files (Unix) for running the benchmark tests.


1) Files of the source program:
   ============================

   tlusty200.f   - contains the whole stellar atmospheres program

  + following 8 files, INCLUDE files to the program:

   ALIPAR.FOR
   ARRAY1.FOR
   ATOMIC.FOR
   BASICS.FOR
   IMPLIC.FOR
   ITERAT.FOR
   MODELP.FOR
   ODFPAR.FOR

The include file have to reside in the same directory as the source
program tlusty200.f; under Unix their names have to be in capital
letter, ie eg. ARRAY1.FOR (not array1.for).

The compilation is done as follows:

Unix - Digital:  f77 -o tlusty200 tlusty200.f
Unix - Sun:      f77 -Nl100 -fast -o tlusty200 tlusty200.f
Linux:           pgf77 -fastsse -Wl,-Bstatic -o tlusty200 tlusty200.f
Linux:           g77 -O3 -static -fno-automatic -o tlusty200 tlusty200.f

VMS:             for tlusty200.f
Unix - Ultrix:   f77 -static -o tlusty200  tlusty200.f
Unicos:          cf77 -Wf"-a static" tlusty200.f

*** g77/gcc is NOT supported in earlier versions***


2) Two IDL useful interface programs:
   ==================================
   
   - PCONV  - plots relative changes (convergence log) - Unit 9 output
              from TLUSTY;
   - PMODEL - plots various quantities of the computed model(s) -
              temperature, electron density, populations. The program
              either plots one or several models in an absolute scale,
              or relatively with respect to a chosen model (i.e. it
              allows an easy graphical comparison of several models).
   Both programs are explained at the beginning in the Comment lines.


3) Test runs:
   ==========

The following files represent all the input and output files involved
in calculating two model atmospheres from the scratch. The selected
benchmark tests are:

A) a moderately sophisticated model with H and He I for Teff=25000, log g = 4.0.

B) analogous models as above, but with including also He II.


The atomic data are not necessarily the best available ones.
So, take the input files as an illustration rather than
the best and recommended set of data.

There are script files Tl (one run of Tlusty - one partial model), and Testrun,
which produces the whole set of benchmarks. Obviously, not all test
cases have to be actually computed.

The models are as follows:

MODEL A:

1. LTE models - started from scratch        - called n25lt.*
2. NLTE/lines - started from LTE            - called n25nl.*

MODEL B:

1. LTE models - started from scratch        - called o25lt.*
2. NLTE/lines - started from LTE            - called o25nl.*



Input files: (see also the User's Guide)

unit 5 - called *.5    - basic data set
unit 8 - called *.8    - starting model (note that the LTE model
         does not need any starting model, since an LTE-gray starting
         model is calculated automatically.)

Output files:
unit 6 - called *.6    - diagnostic output  (NOT SAVED in directory benchtest)
unit 7 - called *.7    - the most important output - contains a condensed
         model atmosphere, to be used for instance as an input to another
         run of TLUSTY (to get a more sophisticated model) or to other
         programs to produce synthetic spectra, etc.
unit 9 - called *.9    - relative changes of physical quantities. This is
         the crucial file to decide whether the model is already converged.
         Changes should generally decrease iteration by iteration, and
         finally should be smaller than, say, 10**-3, at least the
         relative changes of temperature (the maximum, which usually is
         the relative change of the intensity in the first - highest -
         frequency point, may be relatively large due to a great
         sensitivity of the short-wavelength radiation to temperature, but
         that usually does not matter too much).

There is another output file, unit 13, which contains only the table of
emergent fluxes. This table may also be found in unit 6 output; the output
on unit 13 was enabled to be able to easily extract (and plot, if needed)
the information about emergent flux.

The directory also contains another output files, unit 69. They contain
a timing information (for every iteration the time needed for the formal
solution; and for the true linearization). (The actual files are from
the runs on a DEC Alpha XP1000 workstation).
However, it is a Unix-specific feature. To use the program under other
operating systems, then
i) under VMS the corresponding routine - TIMING - has to be commented out.
ii) under Unicos, only FUNCTION second() has to be commented out (it is
    a part of the standard library.)

There are other output files, which are of interest only in special
cases. A brief description is given in corresponding routines.

The user is recommended to consult the attached User's Guide for
details.


